        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-sky_turns_15_per_hour",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The diurnal turn: the sky rotates 15° every hour, so 24 hours close the full 360° circle — 24 × 15 = 360. Right ascension is measured in these hours."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "24 * 15 = 360"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem sky_turns_15_per_hour : 24 * 15 = 360 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-zodiac_ecliptic_360",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The ecliptic band carries twelve signs of 30° each — 12 × 30 = 360 — the Sun's yearly path closed into one circle."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "12 * 30 = 360"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem zodiac_ecliptic_360 : 12 * 30 = 360 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-sexagesimal_arcseconds",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Sexagesimal (Babylonian base-60) measure: 60 arcminutes to a degree and 60 arcseconds to an arcminute give 3600 arcseconds per degree — 60 × 60 = 3600."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "60 * 60 = 3600"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem sexagesimal_arcseconds : 60 * 60 = 3600 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-arcminutes_equator_to_pole",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "One arcminute of latitude is one nautical mile, and the equator-to-pole span is 90° — so 90 × 60 = 5400 arcminutes (5400 nautical miles) from the equator to the pole."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "90 * 60 = 5400"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem arcminutes_equator_to_pole : 90 * 60 = 5400 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-arcminutes_pole_to_pole",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The meridian span from pole to pole is 180° of latitude, so 180 × 60 = 10800 arcminutes (10800 nautical miles) along a meridian from one pole to the other."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "180 * 60 = 10800"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem arcminutes_pole_to_pole : 180 * 60 = 10800 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-arcminutes_full_circle",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A great circle is 360°, and one arcminute of arc is one nautical mile, so 360 × 60 = 21600 arcminutes — a great circle of the earth measures 21600 nautical miles to the arcminute."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "360 * 60 = 21600"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem arcminutes_full_circle : 360 * 60 = 21600 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-longitude_four_minutes_per_degree",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The earth turns 15° of longitude per hour (360° in 24 h), so each degree of longitude is four minutes of time — 15 × 4 = 60, the sixty minutes of an hour shared out one degree at a time."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "15 * 4 = 60"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem longitude_four_minutes_per_degree : 15 * 4 = 60 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-keplers_harmonic_law",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "Kepler's third (harmonic) law, T² = a³, holds exactly in scaled units — the orbits (a,T) = (1,1), (4,8), (9,27) each satisfy T² = a³, the period squared equals the semi-major axis cubed."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "([(1,1),(4,8),(9,27)] : List (Nat × Nat)).all (fun p => p.2^2 == p.1^3)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem keplers_harmonic_law : ([(1,1),(4,8),(9,27)] : List (Nat × Nat)).all (fun p => p.2^2 == p.1^3) := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-metonic_cycle",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The Metonic cycle: 19 solar years fold almost exactly into 235 synodic (lunar) months — 19 × 12 = 228 ordinary months plus 7 intercalary (leap) months = 235. The Sun and Moon realign every 19 years."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "19 * 12 + 7 = 235"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem metonic_cycle : 19 * 12 + 7 = 235 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-great_year_precession",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The classical great year: the equinoxes precess at about 72 years per degree, so the full 360° circuit takes 72 × 360 = 25920 years. (A classical approximation of the ~25772-year platonic year.)"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "72 * 360 = 25920"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem great_year_precession : 72 * 360 = 25920 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page",
          "slug": "theorem-declination_spans_180",
          "children": [
            {
              "type": "heading",
              "tag": "h2",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "A star's fixed coordinate is bounded: declination runs from the south celestial pole −90° to the north +90°, a span of exactly 180° — 90 − (−90) = 180. Celestial latitude is finite, a fixed reference on the sphere."
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "(90 - (-90) : Int) = 180"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "lean",
              "children": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "theorem declination_spans_180 : (90 - (-90) : Int) = 180 := by decide"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
